    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 041750\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV \n950 AM PST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Temperatures will hover within a few degrees of seasonal normals \n  through the work week with otherwise quiet conditions.\n\n* Northerly winds will pick up Friday and Saturday, with stronger\n  speeds and elevated wave heights along the Colorado River\n  Valley.\n\n&&\n\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Tuesday.\n\nExpect dry conditions, temperatures within a few degrees of seasonal \nnormals, and afternoon breeziness through the work week. Shortwave \nridging will persist across the Desert Southwest for another day \nbefore a weak closed low pushes into the central California Coast \nand across the Great Basin on Wednesday. Impacts from this system \nwill be minimal, with only a slight uptick in wind speeds across the \nforecast area (10 to 20 mph gusts in the afternoon, with gusts to\n25 mph in the higher terrain). \n\nHeading into the weekend, heights will increase west-to-east thanks \nto a high pressure system in the eastern Pacific Ocean. This will \nallow temperatures to increase to slightly-above normal. In addition \nto this ridge, a shortwave will move along the upper-level flow and \nwill ultimately cut off in the southeastern Pacific Ocean late-week \ninto the weekend. Associated with this trough axis, an open wave \nwill push southeastward through our forecast area on Saturday. The \nresultant tightened pressure gradients across the region will \nincrease northerly wind gusts on Friday and Saturday to 15 to 25 \nmph. Along the Colorado River Valley, these north winds will be \nenhanced by local topographic features, which will allow speeds to \nrange 30 to 40 mph at times, with wave heights on Lake Mohave and \nLake Havasu to 3 feet. Will continue to assess the need for a wind-\nrelated headline as we get closer. Quiet conditions return for the\nremainder of the weekend into the start of next week.\n&&\n\n\n \n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Winds \nwill remain relatively light from the north to northeast through the \nafternoon, with only a 15 to 20 percent chance of seeing a brief \ngust over 13 knots. Typical southwest to west drainage winds are \nlikely this evening before winds veer more to the north toward \nsunrise. Light winds will continue on Thursday, with primarily \nnortherly directions expected. VFR conditions will prevail through \nThursday, with mainly clear skies today followed by SCT high clouds \nwith bases AOA 20kft AGL Thursday. \n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Overall, light north to \nnorthwest winds are expected across most of the region through the \nTAF period. The exception will be in the Owens and Colorado River \nValleys, where while north winds are expected, occasional gusts to \n20 knots are likely through sunset. Winds will diminish in all areas \novernight and remain light Thursday morning before gusty winds \nreturn to the valleys mentioned above. VFR conditions will prevail \nthrough Thursday, with mainly clear skies today followed by SCT high \nclouds with bases AOA 20kft AGL Thursday. \n\n&&\n\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Soulat\nAVIATION...Planz\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
